1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,298
A zip locks two sides tight.

2
00:00:02,298 --> 00:00:04,615
A ladder with two rails cannot twist.

3
00:00:04,615 --> 00:00:06,464
A double knot will not turn.

4
00:00:06,864 --> 00:00:09,224
A second link locks things in place.

5
00:00:09,224 --> 00:00:12,272
A double bond between two
carbons does the same.

6
00:00:12,722 --> 00:00:16,142
Diya keeps raw bananas next
to ripe ones in the kitchen.

7
00:00:16,142 --> 00:00:17,847
The raw ones ripen faster.

8
00:00:18,197 --> 00:00:22,026
Ripe fruit gives off a gas,
ethene, the simplest alkene.

9
00:00:22,526 --> 00:00:26,302
By the end, you will know how its double
bond is built, why it makes twins,

10
00:00:26,302 --> 00:00:27,608
and how we make alkenes.

11
00:00:28,258 --> 00:00:29,792
So what is an alkene?

12
00:00:30,142 --> 00:00:34,520
An alkene is a hydrocarbon with
one carbon carbon double bond.

13
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:37,293
The general formula is C n H two n.

14
00:00:37,643 --> 00:00:39,381
Names end in e n e.

15
00:00:39,381 --> 00:00:43,256
Ethene has two carbons,
propene three, butene four.

16
00:00:43,606 --> 00:00:47,558
From butene on, one formula can
be arranged in more than one way.

17
00:00:47,558 --> 00:00:49,309
These are structural isomers.

18
00:00:49,709 --> 00:00:54,401
For C four H eight, the double bond
can sit at the end, as in but one ene,

19
00:00:54,401 --> 00:00:56,862
or in the middle, as in but two ene.

20
00:00:57,262 --> 00:01:00,870
Or the chain can branch, as
in two methyl prop one ene.

21
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,025
Now let's build the double
bond on our molecule bench.

22
00:01:05,375 --> 00:01:08,039
Each carbon of the double bond is s p two.

23
00:01:08,039 --> 00:01:12,164
Its three hybrid orbitals lie flat,
at one hundred and twenty degrees.

24
00:01:12,564 --> 00:01:14,577
The two carbons overlap head on.

25
00:01:14,577 --> 00:01:16,262
That makes the sigma bond.

26
00:01:16,662 --> 00:01:19,882
Each carbon still has one
p orbital, standing up.

27
00:01:19,882 --> 00:01:22,756
They overlap side by
side, above and below.

28
00:01:22,756 --> 00:01:24,250
That is the pi bond.

29
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:29,642
Sideways overlap is poor, so the pi
bond is the weaker half, and easy to

30
00:01:29,642 --> 00:01:30,542
attack.

31
00:01:30,736 --> 00:01:34,952
The double bond is shorter, one hundred
and thirty four picometres against

32
00:01:34,952 --> 00:01:39,177
one hundred and fifty four for a
single bond, and stronger, six hundred

33
00:01:39,177 --> 00:01:43,438
and eighty one kilojoules per mole
against three hundred and forty eight.

34
00:01:43,838 --> 00:01:45,329
Now twist one carbon.

35
00:01:45,329 --> 00:01:49,405
The p orbitals turn apart, and
their overlap shrinks to nothing.

36
00:01:49,405 --> 00:01:51,099
The pi bond would break.

37
00:01:51,099 --> 00:01:53,190
So a double bond cannot rotate.

38
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:55,687
No rotation gives twin molecules.

39
00:01:55,687 --> 00:01:56,987
Look at but two ene.

40
00:01:57,387 --> 00:02:01,888
If both methyl groups sit on the
same side of the double bond, we call

41
00:02:01,888 --> 00:02:02,788
it cis.

42
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:05,913
If they sit on opposite
sides, we call it trans.

43
00:02:06,313 --> 00:02:09,007
Same atoms, different
arrangement in space.

44
00:02:09,007 --> 00:02:11,946
This is geometrical, or
cis trans, isomerism.

45
00:02:12,346 --> 00:02:13,589
Pause and think.

46
00:02:13,589 --> 00:02:16,074
Can propene show cis trans isomerism?

47
00:02:19,274 --> 00:02:20,174
No!

48
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:23,650
One carbon of its double bond holds
two hydrogens, so flipping changes

49
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:24,550
nothing.

50
00:02:24,294 --> 00:02:27,691
Each carbon of the double bond
needs two different groups.

51
00:02:28,341 --> 00:02:30,006
Now, how do we make alkenes?

52
00:02:30,006 --> 00:02:31,466
Four ways, link by link.

53
00:02:31,816 --> 00:02:33,537
Link one, from alkynes.

54
00:02:33,537 --> 00:02:38,008
Add one molecule of hydrogen over
palladium on charcoal, slowed down with

55
00:02:38,008 --> 00:02:40,026
sulphur compounds or quinoline.

56
00:02:40,026 --> 00:02:41,797
This is Lindlar's catalyst.

57
00:02:42,547 --> 00:02:44,451
Link two, from alkyl halides.

58
00:02:44,451 --> 00:02:46,119
Heat with alcoholic potash.

59
00:02:46,119 --> 00:02:50,418
It pulls a hydrogen off the next
carbon, the beta carbon, and the halogen

60
00:02:50,418 --> 00:02:51,318
leaves.

61
00:02:51,016 --> 00:02:53,836
This is dehydrohalogenation,
a beta elimination.

62
00:02:54,586 --> 00:02:59,326
The halogen leaves most easily as
iodine, then bromine, then chlorine.

63
00:02:59,326 --> 00:03:03,684
And tertiary halides react fastest,
then secondary, then primary.

64
00:03:04,434 --> 00:03:08,344
Link three, from vicinal dihalides, with
two halogens on neighbouring carbons.

65
00:03:08,344 --> 00:03:10,185
Zinc metal pulls both halogens off.

66
00:03:10,185 --> 00:03:11,418
This is dehalogenation.

67
00:03:12,168 --> 00:03:13,958
Link four, from alcohols.

68
00:03:13,958 --> 00:03:18,346
Heat with concentrated sulphuric
acid, and a water molecule comes out.

69
00:03:18,346 --> 00:03:20,137
This is acidic dehydration.

70
00:03:20,137 --> 00:03:21,577
Ethanol gives ethene.

71
00:03:22,327 --> 00:03:23,345
Remember this.

72
00:03:23,345 --> 00:03:27,602
To make a double bond, pull two
pieces off two neighbouring carbons.

73
00:03:28,302 --> 00:03:29,623
Now, one worked example.

74
00:03:29,623 --> 00:03:32,464
What forms when one two
dibromopropane is treated with zinc?

75
00:03:32,764 --> 00:03:33,664
Step one.

76
00:03:33,491 --> 00:03:36,689
One bromine is on carbon
one, and one on carbon two.

77
00:03:36,689 --> 00:03:39,736
They are neighbours, so
this is a vicinal dihalide.

78
00:03:40,486 --> 00:03:41,386
Step two.

79
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:43,699
Zinc takes both bromines, as zinc bromide.

80
00:03:44,449 --> 00:03:45,349
Step three.

81
00:03:45,346 --> 00:03:48,313
Carbon one and carbon
two each lose one bond.

82
00:03:48,313 --> 00:03:52,041
Those two bonds pair up
between them, as a new pi bond.

83
00:03:52,791 --> 00:03:53,764
Step four.

84
00:03:53,764 --> 00:03:58,341
The product is CH three CH
double bond CH two, propene.

85
00:03:59,091 --> 00:04:02,972
Three carbons, six hydrogens, two
bromines and one zinc on both sides.

86
00:04:02,972 --> 00:04:03,872
Balanced.

87
00:04:04,254 --> 00:04:06,758
One alkyne can give two different twins.

88
00:04:06,758 --> 00:04:08,015
Take but two yne.

89
00:04:08,415 --> 00:04:11,516
With Lindlar's catalyst, both
hydrogens add from the same side.

90
00:04:11,516 --> 00:04:12,864
We get cis but two ene.

91
00:04:13,264 --> 00:04:17,421
With sodium in liquid ammonia, the
hydrogens end up on opposite sides.

92
00:04:17,421 --> 00:04:19,113
We get trans but two ene.

93
00:04:19,513 --> 00:04:24,275
Cis but two ene is slightly polar, with
a dipole moment of zero point three

94
00:04:24,275 --> 00:04:25,237
three debye.

95
00:04:25,237 --> 00:04:28,143
In trans, the pulls cancel to almost zero.

96
00:04:28,543 --> 00:04:32,994
Trans packs better in a solid, so
it melts at a higher temperature.

97
00:04:33,644 --> 00:04:35,105
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:35,455 --> 00:04:37,481
Students think Lindlar's
catalyst gives trans alkenes.

99
00:04:37,881 --> 00:04:38,781
Wrong.

100
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:40,157
Lindlar's catalyst gives cis alkenes.

101
00:04:40,157 --> 00:04:42,402
Sodium in liquid ammonia
gives trans alkenes.

102
00:04:43,052 --> 00:04:44,638
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:44,638 --> 00:04:47,088
Boards ask the preparation methods.

104
00:04:47,088 --> 00:04:51,124
Entrance tests ask which compound
shows cis trans isomerism.

105
00:04:51,724 --> 00:04:52,948
Exam level now.

106
00:04:52,948 --> 00:04:56,350
Which of these compounds
shows cis trans isomerism?

107
00:04:56,350 --> 00:04:57,625
Pause and solve.

108
00:05:03,025 --> 00:05:04,525
The answer is C.

109
00:05:04,525 --> 00:05:07,560
CH three CH double bond CHC l.

110
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:12,781
In C, one carbon holds a methyl and
a hydrogen, the other a chlorine and

111
00:05:12,781 --> 00:05:13,696
a hydrogen.

112
00:05:13,696 --> 00:05:15,684
Two different groups on each.

113
00:05:16,184 --> 00:05:18,188
A has two hydrogens on one carbon.

114
00:05:18,188 --> 00:05:20,192
D has two chlorines on one carbon.

115
00:05:20,692 --> 00:05:23,514
B has two methyl groups on one carbon.

116
00:05:23,514 --> 00:05:27,548
Two same groups on one carbon
means no cis trans isomers.

117
00:05:28,148 --> 00:05:29,688
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:29,688 --> 00:05:31,270
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:34,470 --> 00:05:36,656
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:37,056 --> 00:05:37,956
One.

121
00:05:37,519 --> 00:05:40,371
Why can a carbon carbon
double bond not rotate?

122
00:05:41,171 --> 00:05:42,071
Two.

123
00:05:41,634 --> 00:05:44,667
Which reagent turns an
alkyl halide into an alkene?

124
00:05:45,467 --> 00:05:46,367
Three.

125
00:05:45,967 --> 00:05:48,794
Does Lindlar's catalyst give
a cis or a trans alkene?

126
00:05:49,694 --> 00:05:53,346
Next time: why does a peroxide make
hydrogen bromide add the other way

127
00:05:53,346 --> 00:05:54,246
round?

128
00:05:53,849 --> 00:05:56,941
We find out in reactions of
alkenes and Markovnikov's rule.
